    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"MultiObjective_Transportation\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    I = 5  # sources\n    J = 6  # destinations\n    K = 3  # max suppliers per store\n    w_cost = 0.6\n    w_emission = 0.4\n\n    supplies = [106, 189, 202, 130, 168]\n    demands = [60, 59, 12, 39, 47, 31]\n    cost_matrix = [[17.04, 20.48, 4.6, 9.97, 26.51, 23.08], [15.21, 27.23, 19.28, 13.34, 5.78, 10.4], [17.36, 13.58, 20.19, 21.74, 8.39, 13.89], [3.22, 11.29, 25.3, 15.84, 24.34, 18.19], [21.34, 25.07, 10.55, 23.35, 12.26, 29.35]]\n    emission_matrix = [[10.06, 4.74, 18.21, 11.35, 4.06, 3.87], [15.43, 1.0, 9.74, 15.24, 5.9, 20.0], [6.19, 7.53, 13.64, 12.6, 16.06, 19.54], [18.8, 18.56, 4.79, 5.36, 14.04, 3.45], [1.73, 14.33, 9.17, 13.0, 6.66, 12.13]]\n    fixed_costs = [[225, 130, 124, 231, 260, 150], [170, 101, 85, 205, 83, 292], [104, 243, 131, 235, 248, 241], [198, 252, 56, 216, 263, 94], [85, 192, 130, 75, 97, 126]]\n    moq = [[17, 20, 12, 8, 7, 7], [9, 5, 10, 20, 16, 20], [6, 14, 14, 5, 6, 12], [6, 5, 15, 10, 15, 14], [11, 8, 12, 20, 7, 15]]\n\n    # Big-M per route\n    total_demand = sum(demands)\n    big_M = [[min(supplies[i], total_demand) for j in range(J)] for i in range(I)]\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Continuous: shipment quantities\n    x = {}\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            x[(i,j)] = m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # Binary: route-open indicators\n    y = {}\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            y[(i,j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"RouteOpen_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # --- Objective ---\n    # Weighted variable cost + fixed setup costs\n    m.setObjective(\n        quicksum(\n            (w_cost * cost_matrix[i][j] + w_emission * emission_matrix[i][j]) * x[(i,j)]\n            for i in range(I) for j in range(J)\n        )\n        + quicksum(fixed_costs[i][j] * y[(i,j)] for i in range(I) for j in range(J)),\n        GRB.MINIMIZE\n    )\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # C1: Supply\n    for i in range(I):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(x[(i,j)] for j in range(J)) <= supplies[i],\n            name=f\"Supply_S{i}\")\n\n    # C2: Demand\n    for j in range(J):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(x[(i,j)] for i in range(I)) >= demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_D{j}\")\n\n    # C3: Linking \u2014 can only ship if route is open\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            m.addConstr(\n                x[(i,j)] <= big_M[i][j] * y[(i,j)],\n                name=f\"Link_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # C4: Minimum order quantity\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            m.addConstr(\n                x[(i,j)] >= moq[i][j] * y[(i,j)],\n                name=f\"MOQ_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # C5: Max suppliers per store\n    for j in range(J):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(y[(i,j)] for i in range(I)) <= K,\n            name=f\"MaxSupplier_D{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Memo\nTo: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Sustainability & Logistics Director\nSubject: Shipping plan that balances transportation cost and carbon impact under operational realities\nDate:\n\nContext\nWe ship from multiple warehouses to multiple stores across our network. Every lane between a warehouse and a store carries two important signals: a dollar cost per unit and a carbon emission rate per unit. In practice, the least-expensive options tend to be the most carbon intensive, while cleaner alternatives can come at a higher price. We need a planning approach that reflects both our financial discipline and our sustainability commitments, while dealing with the realities of how freight gets contracted and received in the field.\n\nOperational rules we must respect\n- Route activation costs: Starting up any shipping lane triggers a one-time setup fee tied to contracts, onboarding, and dock readiness. A lane is either active or not; if active, this fee is incurred regardless of volume moved.\n- Minimum order quantities: Once a lane is activated, we must move at least a specified number of units on that lane to justify carrier and dock utilization. Partial-load exceptions are not available.\n- Supplier limits at stores: Each store can receive from only a limited number of warehouses to stay within receiving capacity, scheduling windows, and vendor management limits. This keeps yard congestion and administrative complexity in check.\n\nSupply and demand coverage\n- Shipments from each warehouse cannot exceed the available inventory at that location.\n- Each store\u2019s requirement must be fully covered through the combination of its inbound lanes, while adhering to the supplier limit noted above.\n\nManagement priorities\nLeadership has set relative importance weights for cost and emissions. We will combine the variable shipping spend and the total carbon impact using these weights to guide trade-offs. The one-time setup fees for any activated lanes are added in full on top of this weighted blend; they are not adjusted by the weights.\n\nPlanning goal\nSelect which lanes to activate and determine the shipment quantities on each active lane so that:\n- We do not exceed warehouse inventory and we meet every store\u2019s requirement.\n- Any active lane moves at least its minimum order quantity.\n- Each store receives from no more than the allowed number of warehouses.\n- We achieve the best achievable balance of total spend and total emissions under the management weights, while recognizing that lane setup fees are incurred in full when lanes are activated.\n\nThis approach will give us a pragmatic, field-executable shipping plan that transparently trades off financial and environmental outcomes while respecting the way our network actually operates.\n\nANNEX \u2013 DATA\n- Number of warehouses: 5\n- Number of stores: 6\n- Warehouse supplies (units): Warehouse capacities: [106, 189, 202, 130, 168]\n- Store demands (units): Store demand requirements: [60, 59, 12, 39, 47, 31]\n- Per\u2011unit shipping cost matrix ($/unit): Shipping Costs ($/unit, 5 sources x 6 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4 | D5\n-----------------------------------------\nS0         |  17.04 |  20.48 |   4.60 |   9.97 |  26.51 |  23.08\nS1         |  15.21 |  27.23 |  19.28 |  13.34 |   5.78 |  10.40\nS2         |  17.36 |  13.58 |  20.19 |  21.74 |   8.39 |  13.89\nS3         |   3.22 |  11.29 |  25.30 |  15.84 |  24.34 |  18.19\nS4         |  21.34 |  25.07 |  10.55 |  23.35 |  12.26 |  29.35\n- Per\u2011unit emission matrix (kg CO2/unit): CO2 Emissions (kg CO2/unit, 5 sources x 6 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4 | D5\n-----------------------------------------\nS0         |  10.06 |   4.74 |  18.21 |  11.35 |   4.06 |   3.87\nS1         |  15.43 |   1.00 |   9.74 |  15.24 |   5.90 |  20.00\nS2         |   6.19 |   7.53 |  13.64 |  12.60 |  16.06 |  19.54\nS3         |  18.80 |  18.56 |   4.79 |   5.36 |  14.04 |   3.45\nS4         |   1.73 |  14.33 |   9.17 |  13.00 |   6.66 |  12.13\n- Importance weight on cost: 60%\n- Importance weight on emissions: 40%\n- Route setup costs ($): Route Setup Costs ($, 5 sources x 6 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4 | D5\n-----------------------------------------\nS0         |    225 |    130 |    124 |    231 |    260 |    150\nS1         |    170 |    101 |     85 |    205 |     83 |    292\nS2         |    104 |    243 |    131 |    235 |    248 |    241\nS3         |    198 |    252 |     56 |    216 |    263 |     94\nS4         |     85 |    192 |    130 |     75 |     97 |    126\n- Minimum order quantities per active route (units): Minimum Order Quantities (units, 5 sources x 6 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4 | D5\n-----------------------------------------\nS0         |     17 |     20 |     12 |      8 |      7 |      7\nS1         |      9 |      5 |     10 |     20 |     16 |     20\nS2         |      6 |     14 |     14 |      5 |      6 |     12\nS3         |      6 |      5 |     15 |     10 |     15 |     14\nS4         |     11 |      8 |     12 |     20 |      7 |     15\n- Maximum suppliers per store: 3",
